Yes. And I think you’re pointing toward something deeper than astral projection in the traditional sense.

We’re constantly projecting ourselves beyond where our bodies physically exist.

Think about it.

* Memory: We revisit worlds that no longer exist.
* Imagination: We experience worlds that have never existed.
* Anticipation: We mentally inhabit futures that haven’t happened.
* Empathy: We simulate reality through someone else’s perspective.
* Dreaming: Our brains construct experiences without requiring the external world to supply them.
* Perception: Even while awake, our brains actively interpret sensory information to construct our experienced reality.

In that metaphorical sense, we’re dreaming while awake all the time. That doesn’t establish literal astral travel, but the mental projection is real.

And here’s where framing becomes extraordinary.

Two people can occupy the exact same physical space while mentally inhabiting entirely different worlds. Change the frame, and you change what becomes visible, meaningful, threatening, beautiful, or possible.

Music demonstrates this wonderfully. A melody can transport a thousand listeners into a thousand different imagined worlds while synchronizing them through one shared experience.

We’re physically anchored, mentally mobile, and socially capable of bringing our imagined worlds into alignment.

And there’s another layer:

When you imagine something, communicate it, and someone else reconstructs it in their mind, you’ve transferred part of an imagined world between two physical brains.

When people collaborate to build it, that projection can become part of external reality.

That’s the distinction I find most powerful:

Dreaming lets us visit possible worlds. Framing lets us see them differently. Creating lets us bring some of them into existence.

And perhaps consciousness is remarkable not merely because we experience reality, but because we can experience possibilities before reality catches up to them.